Tell every plugin which company the host actually checked

The four plugin bridge routes (bridge/data, bridge/action, data/:key, actions/:key) now tell the plugin worker which company the host actually checked. Before this, the route validated body.companyId but forwarded body.params untouched, so a member of company A could send companyId A (which passes the access check) and params.companyId B, and a plugin that reads params.companyId would act on company B. Each route now captures the validated company from assertPluginBridgeScope, gives a params.companyId that is a non-empty string and differs from it the same access check the body company got (403 when the caller cannot reach that company, otherwise the call goes through with params.companyId untouched, which is what the instance-level email-tools and help-scout rules pages need), and calls the worker with params.hostScope set to { companyId (null for an instance-admin global call), userId (the signed-in board user) }, overwriting anything the browser put under that key. Plugins that send the same company in both places (email-tools, phone-tools) pass through unchanged. The two action routes also write one activity-log row (plugin.action.performed, with the action key, idempotencyKey and locationKey) after a successful company-scoped call, skipped for global calls and for failures; an audit-write failure is logged rather than failing a call whose side effect already happened. protocol.ts documents params.hostScope as a host-reserved key that plugins read and never send. The authz test file gains 29 cases covering the stamp on all four routes, the overwrite, the 403 when the claimed company is out of reach, the pass-through when it is not, the pass-through on match, viewer and agent 403s, and the activity row rules; the existing admin global-call expectation is updated to include the null-company stamp. Note for the orchestrator: the working tree also holds unrelated modified and untracked files under packages/shared (start-work validators) and scripts/_run-with-clean-env.mjs from other work; this item did not touch them.

